WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.120
- You don't drink coffee?

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.113
- [Nick] No,

00:00:02.113 --> 00:00:03.943
- I don't trust anybody
who doesn't drink coffee.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.341
This interview's over.

00:00:07.341 --> 00:00:10.180
- My name is Nick Barili and
I'm traveling the country

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.430
to sit down with some of
the biggest Latinx artists

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.780
in places that have meaning to them

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.020
and help tell their stories.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.110
We'll talk about their
journeys and careers

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.050
and explore issues of identity, culture,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.100
discrimination, and representation.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.247
This is "SEEN".

00:00:23.351 --> 00:00:25.934
(upbeat music)

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.200
Hey everyone, we're back.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.930
I am on campus at Cal State
Northridge, also known as CSUN.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.100
I am on my way to meet Eva
Longoria, a true multi hyphenate.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.970
Actress, director, producer,
entrepreneur, philanthropist.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.180
So many different hats.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.010
The place she chose for us to
meet is Jerome Richfield Hall.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.886
Let's go find out why.

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:55.265
(upbeat music)

00:00:55.265 --> 00:00:56.206
- Hello, hello!

00:00:56.206 --> 00:00:57.039
- How are you doing?

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:57.872
Pleasure to meet you.

00:00:57.872 --> 00:00:58.760
- How are you?

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.647
- I'm good, how are you?

00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:01.580
- Good.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.576
- So Jerome Richfield Hall,
of all the places in LA

00:01:04.576 --> 00:01:07.399
that we've could have met, why
is this the place you picked?

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:08.232
- You know what?

00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:09.570
CSUN is a very special place for me.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.210
It's where I got my masters.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.910
And this particular building
is the Chicano studies hall.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.700
And CSUN was the first university

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.159
to have a Chicano studies program.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:21.338
- Wow, I didn't know that.

00:01:21.338 --> 00:01:22.350
- It's the first in the country

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.670
and so it's the best in the country.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.600
This place was probably the
place I grew most in my life.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.110
- What does it feel like
to be on campus again

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.873
after 10 years ago or 15 years ago

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.833
when you got your masters?

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:38.833
- I'm not that old Nick.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.699
No, but it was 10 years ago.

00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:42.652
(laughs)

00:01:42.652 --> 00:01:44.470
- All right, great, I
thought I did my research.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.880
- But its not 15 years ago.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.203
But actually makes me exhausted
'cause I was exhausted

00:01:48.203 --> 00:01:50.123
the whole time I was here.

00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:51.798
- You were actually attending here

00:01:51.798 --> 00:01:53.100
while you were working on "Housewives"?

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.270
- Yeah, yeah, I did night school.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.860
I would finish filming and
come here 7 to 10 at night

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.800
and then go home and then do it all again.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.490
Be on set at 5 AM and it fills my heart

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.930
with my Mexican pride.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.560
- Well I wanna talk more
about what it was like

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.410
during those days and why
you picked Chicano studies.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.280
But let's go inside.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.709
- Okay, come inside 'cause
there's the most beautiful mural

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:17.322
that made my heart swell
every time I saw it.

00:02:17.322 --> 00:02:19.905
(upbeat music)

00:02:24.672 --> 00:02:27.630
Oh my God, it smells the same.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:28.463
- Really?

00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:30.710
- Look, so this is Ballet Folklorico.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.310
I always wanted to do it as a kid.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:33.610
We couldn't afford to do it.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.120
My mom was like, "It's too expensive",

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.670
and so when I got my masters,
we had to pick an art.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.510
I chose Ballet Folklorico and I danced.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:47.240
Oh my God, this is my adviser,
Christina Ayala-Alcantar.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.420
She's amazing, she's still here.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.211
But I wanna show you the mural.

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:54.794
(upbeat music)

00:02:58.185 --> 00:02:59.630
- Tada!

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.116
- Wow, this is the mural
you were talking about.

00:03:01.116 --> 00:03:02.821
- This is the mural.

00:03:02.821 --> 00:03:03.960
(upbeat music)

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.150
- That's Dr. Acūna.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.970
He's the godfather of Chicano studies.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.850
His book is what made me
wanna study Chicano studies,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.440
was called "Occupied America".

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.640
And it was the history of our people.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.167
- It's crazy that growing up in Texas

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.190
and I grew up in
California that that's not

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.170
a part of our curriculum.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.215
- That's part of the
struggle with Chicano studies

00:03:24.215 --> 00:03:28.290
is getting our community
to learn our history.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.123
It's a fight.

00:03:29.123 --> 00:03:31.710
They're taking it away
in Arizona and Texas.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.160
I mean, they're saying
you should not learn this.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.170
And so that's part of this
whole movement is to make sure

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:40.614
that we do learn our history.

00:03:40.614 --> 00:03:43.197
(upbeat music)

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.910
- I met you briefly like 10 years ago.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.790
Sure you don't remember,
but it was at Beso

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.260
and you were honoring the farm workers

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.876
in Dolores that night.

00:03:52.876 --> 00:03:53.908
- [Eva] Yes.

00:03:53.908 --> 00:03:55.380
And I was so inspired that
at a moment in your career

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.189
when all the light was on you,

00:03:57.189 --> 00:04:00.682
you took that light and
used it to empower others.

00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:01.942
What point in your career
did that really become

00:04:01.942 --> 00:04:03.320
a mission for you?

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.240
- It's probably when I moved to LA.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.420
The pride in California is very different.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.110
Like the word Chicano
comes from California,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:14.062
the movement originated in California.

00:04:14.062 --> 00:04:16.370
And one of the first people
I met was Dolores Huerta .

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:17.203
- [Nick] Oh wow.

00:04:17.203 --> 00:04:18.740
- Yeah, I wasn't famous.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:22.140
I was just asking her
questions about her legacy

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.200
and her work.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.200
And she said, "You know, one
day you're gonna have a voice

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.454
so you better have something to say",

00:04:28.454 --> 00:04:30.500
and I never forgot that.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.940
And then you know, later on,
to actually have that platform

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.250
to be able to bring attention
to causes that are near

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.243
and dear to me has been an honor.

00:04:40.255 --> 00:04:42.672
(slow music)

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.030
- [Nick] Would love to
go into the classroom.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.210
- Yes!

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.510
As tired as I used to be
walking into a classroom,

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:56.203
the minute I walked in, I was
like, "I'm going to learn!"

00:04:56.203 --> 00:04:58.720
I was like, that's the benefit
of being an older student

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.180
is that you're eager and you're curious,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.397
and you're like a sponge
as opposed to being like 21

00:05:04.397 --> 00:05:06.777
and you're like, "I
just want to graduate".

00:05:06.777 --> 00:05:08.026
(laughs)

00:05:08.026 --> 00:05:10.609
(upbeat music)

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.180
- If you had to look back,

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.730
what do you think that time
taught you about yourself?

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.120
- You know, that you're
capable of it anything.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.650
I mean, the reality is you
have more time in the day

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:26.560
than you think.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.867
It's the amount of time
you waste in the day.

00:05:28.867 --> 00:05:30.050
And the other thing it taught me was

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.510
you're never too old to learn.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:35.180
You're never too old to
finish something or to go back

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.880
and do something or to try something new.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.810
And so that's what really,
I think the biggest message

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.610
that came out of it was like,
I was, "I'm gonna do this.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.262
I can do this, I did it". You know?

00:05:45.262 --> 00:05:49.050
- How did that set you up for
all your future endeavors?

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.930
Because now you're multi hyphenate.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.960
Not only do you act,
you direct, you produce,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.320
you're entrepreneur,
you're philanthropist.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.510
- I think for the most part, like I said,

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.380
my thesis became the
basis of my foundation.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.350
And so to have research really
backing your initiatives

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.850
to say I studied what our community needs

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:08.740
and that's where I'm going
to focus my energy on

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.530
'cause I want to make
sustainable effective change.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:14.210
That's where it probably
helped me the most.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.690
It also made me a little bit more credible

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.240
my political activism.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.700
Like I knew what I was talking about.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.840
I knew the history, I knew the policies.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.830
And I continue to stay educated on that

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.560
because the fight never
ends for our community.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.220
And so it's done a lot for me.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.360
- I heard that one of the things

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.230
that powered you through was coffee.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.334
- Yeah.

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:36.150
- I haven't had coffee
since I was a PA in college.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.462
- You don't drink coffee?

00:06:37.462 --> 00:06:38.295
- [Nick] No,

00:06:38.295 --> 00:06:40.063
- I don't trust anybody
who doesn't drink coffee.

00:06:41.683 --> 00:06:42.783
This interview's over.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:44.783
This interview's over.

00:06:44.783 --> 00:06:46.842
Come and see my coffee machine.

00:06:46.842 --> 00:06:49.031
(curious music)

00:06:49.031 --> 00:06:50.697
.Tada!

00:06:50.697 --> 00:06:51.530
- So this is the actual coffee
machine when you were here?

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:54.260
- Yes! This was my coffee spot you guys.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.610
It tasted like shit, I can't
believe this is still here.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:59.940
I don't think they've
changed out the coffee since,

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.660
but I would take-

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:02.493
- Are we doing this?

00:07:02.493 --> 00:07:03.326
- Let's buy one.

00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:04.440
But yeah, oh gosh.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.700
It takes credit cards.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.110
I don't think it took credit cards back.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.510
Maybe it takes Apple pay now.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.533
Totally takes Apple pay!

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.990
- Its not connected to the internet?

00:07:15.537 --> 00:07:17.393
(laughs)

00:07:17.393 --> 00:07:18.524
- We can't Apple pay.

00:07:18.524 --> 00:07:19.357
- This gonna be the whole video.

00:07:19.357 --> 00:07:20.800
It's just two people trying to get coffee.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.700
- Y'all we've gotta taste this coffee.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.646
So look what happens.

00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:28.050
- [Nick] Wow.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.320
(laughs)

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.700
- All right, let's see.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.533
Okay.

00:07:32.533 --> 00:07:33.366
Oh man!

00:07:34.807 --> 00:07:36.070
- Takes you back?

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.664
- Smell that, you don't care.

00:07:38.664 --> 00:07:39.840
- Smells like coffee.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.650
- See, to me it smells like late nights.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:43.810
That's what it smells like.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.290
All right, we gotta get you
one and then we should go

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.160
have our coffee break.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.850
I'll show you where I
actually used to sit outside.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.198
- Would love to.

00:07:50.198 --> 00:07:52.781
(upbeat music)

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:59.532
Welcome to the courtyard.

00:07:59.532 --> 00:08:00.400
- So this is where sometimes

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.670
you would hang out before class?

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:02.503
- Sometimes.

00:08:02.503 --> 00:08:05.160
I was usually rushing,
but the times I did get

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.810
to have a little bit of time, this was it.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.927
It was crowded every
single seat was taken.

00:08:10.927 --> 00:08:12.330
(upbeat music)

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.190
- Let's take it back.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.030
I would love to get a little
bit of context in your life.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.700
I always love in interviews

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.110
pretending that it's a movie of your life.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:21.000
- Who's playing me?

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.194
- You're playing you.

00:08:22.194 --> 00:08:23.484
- I'm playing me.

00:08:23.484 --> 00:08:25.690
Oh my God, I've always
wanted me to play me!

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.240
(laughs)

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.380
- That opening scene that
comes into Corpus Christi

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.840
interior childhood home.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.328
What are we seeing?

00:08:33.328 --> 00:08:34.161
What are we hearing?

00:08:34.161 --> 00:08:34.994
What are we smelling?

00:08:34.994 --> 00:08:35.827
- I'm from Corpus Christi.

00:08:35.827 --> 00:08:36.800
But I was really raised on the ranch,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.920
which is outside of Corpus Christi.

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.900
So we grew up with cows
and chickens and pigs.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.063
Yeah, I mean we were from the ranch.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.220
My swimming pool was
the cow trough of water.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.950
That's where we're like, "We're so lucky"!

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:52.870
We have a swimming pool.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.689
Corpus Christi is a really
small town in Texas.

00:08:56.689 --> 00:08:58.870
I grew up in a Mexican neighborhood.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:08:59.770
And so I remember,

00:09:00.954 --> 00:09:05.090
never really feeling Mexican
'cause there was no need

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:07.590
to say it, we were all just
in that neighborhood together.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.910
And it wasn't until the third grade,

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.930
when I tested into a gifted
and talented program,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.700
so I had to go to a
school way across town.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:17.630
- Take me to that first day,

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:19.200
that first day you're getting on the bus.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.110
- On the school bus.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:21.630
- On the school bus to go there.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.400
- I remember getting on the
school bus and every morning

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.800
to this day - this morning
I ate this breakfast.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.310
This is the same
breakfast I eat every day.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:30.650
It was a bean taco, no cheese,

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.640
just a flour tortilla and beans.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.160
Every morning I eat for breakfast.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.080
And my mom would make it for
me and I'd take it to school

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.498
and I'd eat it.

00:09:37.498 --> 00:09:38.900
And so I got on the bus with my bean taco.

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:42.800
Everybody was staring at me
and everybody had a pop tart.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.250
Everybody had one and I go,
"Oh my God, what is that?"

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.660
They looked at me and
they're like, "What's that?"

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.160
And I was like, "It's the bean taco?

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:50.672
Don't you get it?

00:09:50.672 --> 00:09:51.505
Doesn't everybody eat a bean taco?"

00:09:51.505 --> 00:09:52.470
And they were like, "No."

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:55.107
And I remember a little
girl on the bus going,

00:09:55.107 --> 00:09:56.880
"She's Mexican."

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.000
And I didn't know what that meant.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.429
I didn't know the word,
I was like, "What?"

00:10:00.429 --> 00:10:03.330
So that was the first
time I felt like other.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.129
I was like, "Oh, oh, okay."

00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:07.739
And I think there was like two
Mexicans in the whole school.

00:10:07.739 --> 00:10:09.760
- So you come home from school that day?

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:10.593
- Yeah.

00:10:10.593 --> 00:10:12.550
- What's that conversation
like with your mom?

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:15.100
- Oh, I begged my mom to buy pop tarts.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.468
I was like, "Mom, you gotta buy pop tarts.

00:10:17.468 --> 00:10:18.910
I don't wanna bean taco anymore."

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:20.840
She was like, "We're not buying pop tarts.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:22.950
Here's two bean tacos for tomorrow."

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.840
And so yeah, I just
remember going, oh my gosh,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.170
I'm different and I didn't know why.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.330
- What was it like at that moment

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:31.900
kind of straddling both worlds?

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.250
I mean, you're in a neighborhood

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:34.330
that's predominantly Mexican.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:35.163
You're going to school

00:10:35.163 --> 00:10:36.800
that's predominantly affluent and white.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.503
- I also think it's confusing.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.270
to them, by the way, not to me,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:43.230
because I didn't have an accent.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.440
I'm white passing.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.100
So they're like, "You don't look Mexican,

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.160
you don't sound Mexican."

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:50.559
And it was kind of the same reception

00:10:50.559 --> 00:10:53.830
I had when I came to Hollywood.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.480
Yeah, they were like, "Oh,

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.350
so could you do that with an accent?"

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.220
And I'd be like, "I don't have an accent"

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.820
or "Could you do it in Spanish?"

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.410
And I didn't speak Spanish.

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:04.580
And so they're like,
"Well, how are you Hispanic

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:05.413
and not speak Spanish?"

00:11:05.413 --> 00:11:07.970
I'm like, "There's a lot of
us that don't speak Spanish."

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.160
I've since learned it

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.070
but at that moment coming to Hollywood,

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:12.710
I would never get the Latin roles

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:14.110
'cause I wasn't Latin enough.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.500
And then I wouldn't get the white roles

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.548
because I was too Latin.

00:11:17.548 --> 00:11:19.583
I played a lot of Italians.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.830
- Speak about your parents' decision

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.790
to not teach you Spanish
in an attempt to assimilate

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:28.383
because I feel like that
happened to a lot of people

00:11:28.383 --> 00:11:30.540
that grew up here.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:31.840
- Yeah, especially in Texas.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:33.640
I'm ninth generation American.

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:35.977
I never crossed a border,
the border crossed us.

00:11:35.977 --> 00:11:39.510
And so when people go,
"Go home!", we're like,

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:40.930
"No, we are home."

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:42.205
- This is home.

00:11:42.205 --> 00:11:43.038
- This is home.

00:11:43.038 --> 00:11:44.590
My family was under five different flags

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:45.740
without ever moving.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.660
From Spain to Mexico, to France,

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.200
to Republic of Texas, to America.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.490
And so for a lot of Texans
assimilation was key.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:57.060
Assimilation was important.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:58.530
So my parents felt that they said,

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.640
"We don't want you to
be discriminated against

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.180
because you speak Spanish."

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.560
And so they spoke Spanish to each other

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:05.640
but they wouldn't speak it to us.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:08.350
And we were an English only household.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.410
And I never learned it
until my adult life,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.392
until I married a Mexican.

00:12:13.392 --> 00:12:14.260
(laughs)

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.250
- When was the first time
that you turned on a TV

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:20.180
or watched a movie that
you felt seen as a Latina

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:21.800
and you felt proud to be Latina?

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.020
- It was probably like
Salma Hayek in Desperados

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.200
or it was like pretty late in life.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.200
I remember Rita Moreno
right in West Side Story,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.940
but I wasn't Puerto Rican so
I kinda didn't understand it.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.340
I was like, "Oh! Mm, no."

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:41.507
And they brown faced them
on that original movie.

00:12:41.507 --> 00:12:43.530
And I remember even Rita
talking about it of like,

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.100
"Why are you making us so dark?"

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.430
And I just thought, I
actually remember thinking

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.050
"They're a weird color."

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:52.560
But yeah, growing up,
it was very, very few.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.992
- Yeah, same when I first saw "La Bamba",

00:12:54.992 --> 00:12:56.592
that was like the first time I was like...

00:12:56.592 --> 00:12:59.980
And I was a Mexican but I was
like the Latino experience

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:00.920
I could relate to.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.760
- So it was like, but you
know I was lucky growing up

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.590
in Corpus with Selena.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.220
Selena was one or two years older than me.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:10.079
And so growing up with her
and seeing her trajectory,

00:13:10.079 --> 00:13:13.820
we used to see her play at
quinceaneras and weddings,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.250
so it wasn't a big deal.

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:18.030
But once she started
blowing up and becoming big,

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.230
I thought that, I really
saw myself in Selena

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.450
because she was a "Texican" like I was.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:26.300
And she really embraced being Latina.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:28.090
And so it was just like,
I was like, "Yes, her,

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:29.590
I want to be like her!", you know?

00:13:30.512 --> 00:13:32.028
- Right, speaking of quinceaneras.

00:13:32.028 --> 00:13:33.170
- Quinceaneras.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.193
- My accent come and goes.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:39.628
Wanting to do yours, you
went to go work at Wendy's.

00:13:39.628 --> 00:13:40.716
- Yes.

00:13:40.716 --> 00:13:41.549
- What was that like?

00:13:41.549 --> 00:13:44.740
- Yes, it was crazy
because I wasn't old enough

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:45.573
to get a job.

00:13:45.573 --> 00:13:48.120
So I stole my sister's ID and
I went and applied and got it.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:49.960
And I didn't wanna tell
my parents cause I knew

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.050
they'd get mad.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:55.160
I worked there for like two
weeks without them knowing

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.480
until one of my teachers
came in and she saw me

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.912
and she told my parents like-

00:14:00.912 --> 00:14:02.140
- She's a little young to be working.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:03.410
- She's a little young to be working.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.570
And my mom was like,
"Did you go get a job?"

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:06.403
And I was like, "Yes".

00:14:06.403 --> 00:14:07.610
And so, I mean, it was legal.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.350
All she had to do was
I had to get permission

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:10.710
from my parents to work.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.920
So I was like, "Mom, please,
I want a quinceanera"

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.254
because they wouldn't pay for it.

00:14:14.254 --> 00:14:16.240
My mom and dad were like "Your sister

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.530
didn't get a quinceanera, you're
not getting a quinceanera."

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:21.320
I mean, they're weddings,
they're as expensive as weddings.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.950
And I was like, "I want a quinceanera."

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.690
So I got a job to make my own quinceanera

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:27.320
And I did that, yes.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:28.770
- [Nick] Where does that
work ethic come from?

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:31.056
- My work ethic comes from my mom.

00:14:31.056 --> 00:14:33.151
My mom is like, I still to this day,

00:14:33.151 --> 00:14:35.560
think I don't do enough because
I just see what my mom did

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.470
without a cell phone and
without all the technology

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:39.510
that we have today, she's super.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.628
I don't know how she did it.

00:14:41.628 --> 00:14:42.606
She was always there.

00:14:42.606 --> 00:14:46.080
And so I don't remember a
day without my mom working

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:47.190
as hard as she could.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.520
And she was always very efficient.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:50.330
My mom would say, "I'm
gonna go into the bank.

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:51.950
You're gonna go into the
store, get the tomatoes,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.070
I'll meet you with the cash register."

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.450
I mean, she got everything done.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.080
She maximized the day.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:57.310
- She was a natural producer.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:58.690
I think about that from my mom too.

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:00.030
I mean, my mom was a single mom,

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:02.070
went to school and she was a professor

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.850
and she did all this
stuff and she had a job

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:05.600
and then she came home and
helped me with my homework.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:06.870
And I was like, I look back and I'm like,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.060
how did you have time to do all that

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:10.180
and still be present in my life?

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.583
- Yeah, super moms, it
was a different era.

00:15:12.583 --> 00:15:15.210
- What was the point
where you started to think

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.430
that being an actress
or coming to Hollywood

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.662
was something that you wanted for you?

00:15:19.662 --> 00:15:22.140
- It was overnight one day.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:25.010
I didn't dream of it, I
didn't grow up with it.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:26.820
We never went to the
movies when I was young.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.640
We didn't have that, oh,
look at the movie stars.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.950
Celebrity culture wasn't a thing.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.180
And so role models growing up
for me was my family, my mom,

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:36.600
my sister, my aunt.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.670
I didn't really look
up to Hollywood people.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:39.670
I didn't even know what that meant.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.400
And I'd never really
been outside of Texas.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:46.250
And so in college I ran out
of money my senior year.

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:48.130
I was like, "I want to
finish, I want to finish."

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:49.027
So my friend was like,

00:15:49.027 --> 00:15:52.686
"Hey, you should join
the scholarship pageant."

00:15:52.686 --> 00:15:53.519
And I was like, "What's that?"

00:15:53.519 --> 00:15:55.070
And she said, "It's like a beauty pageant,

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:56.860
but you get a scholarship if you win."

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:00.810
And I remember like, I just
wanted to get fourth place

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.578
because fourth place was like books.

00:16:03.578 --> 00:16:04.750
- Cover your school.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:07.990
- So I was like, "If I
could just get fourth place,

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:09.950
okay, that'll be taken care of."

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:12.960
And so they called,
"Fourth place is Jane."

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:16.850
And I was like, "Oh man, now
I'm not gonna win anything."

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.510
And then the third place, and
I won, I won the whole thing.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:20.800
And I was like, "Oh my
God, this is amazing."

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:23.164
So I was happy 'cause I
got to pay my senior year.

00:16:23.164 --> 00:16:25.770
But because I won that pageant,
I had to be in another one.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:27.707
And I was like, "Oh no, I don't wanna-"

00:16:27.707 --> 00:16:29.646
- Right, I'm just trying
to pay for college.

00:16:29.646 --> 00:16:30.930
- I just wanted to be in this one.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:32.470
Then I had to go to the next one

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:34.860
which was miss Corpus Christi and I won.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.250
My mom was the one that
was like, "That's it.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:38.120
No more pageants.

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:41.490
This is not the way I want you to go."

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.250
But in my prize package
for miss Corpus Christi

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.420
was a trip to Los Angeles.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:47.920
I just thought, "Oh, that'd be fun."

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.040
I didn't think "I'm gonna
be an actor", like nothing.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.830
One of the reasons to come was
this like talent competition.

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.292
I was like, "Yeah yeah, I'll
do this talent competition."

00:16:55.292 --> 00:16:58.340
But I really just wanted to
go to Hollywood Boulevard

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:00.700
and walk around and see Santa Monica pier.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:03.920
When I went into the talent competition,

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:07.300
I won everything and all
these managers and agents

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:08.690
wanted to "sign" me.

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.470
I was like, "What do you mean sign?"

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:11.840
I don't even know what that means.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:16.280
And it was 1998, which like
Ricky mania, J-Lo mania.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:19.870
It was like, to be Latina in
the industry in that moment,

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:21.500
a lot of doors were opening.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.884
So all these agents were
like, "This would be great."

00:17:23.884 --> 00:17:25.950
I was like, "What do you mean?"

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:27.388
I didn't even know.

00:17:27.388 --> 00:17:28.764
- They had plans for
you you didn't have yet.

00:17:28.764 --> 00:17:31.200
- They had plans and dreams
for me I didn't even know of.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:35.470
And I was lucky, I signed
with somebody good.

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.690
And I was like, literally
called my mom on that trip

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:39.030
and I said, "Hey, I'm gonna stay in LA.

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:39.863
I'm gonna be an actor."

00:17:39.863 --> 00:17:41.759
Just like that one day, one day.

00:17:41.759 --> 00:17:43.963
And she goes, "Okay..."

00:17:43.963 --> 00:17:47.230
Because I had just finished
college, my mom was like,

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:48.063
"Well, you'll be fine.

00:17:48.063 --> 00:17:49.860
You can get a job anywhere,
you have your degree now."

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.620
And I said, "Yeah, yeah,
I'll just go get a job."

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:52.640
That was it literally one day.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.495
And I just never went home.

00:17:54.495 --> 00:17:55.675
- Wow.

00:17:55.675 --> 00:18:00.250
- I had $23 in my bank
account and I was like,

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:01.380
"Okay, I need a job."

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.760
And I went to a temp agency to get a job.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:06.500
And the temp agency ended
up hiring me to work there.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:08.383
- As a head hunter.

00:18:08.383 --> 00:18:09.500
- As a head hunter, yeah.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:10.618
- Which you ended up keeping

00:18:10.618 --> 00:18:12.350
even after you got young and restless.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.812
- Yeah. 'cause I made more
money head hunting than acting.

00:18:14.812 --> 00:18:17.080
And so I was like, "Oh, okay,

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:19.110
let me just keep doing
this to pay my rent."

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.540
And I would do my head hunting job

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.730
out of my dressing room at CBS.

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.100
And I would be on the
phone and on my computer.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.350
And then they're like,
"Eva, camera's ready."

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:28.510
And I'm like "All right!"

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:31.690
And I also had to hide the
fact that I was an actor

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:32.700
from the corporate world,

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.970
'cause they always frowned upon actors.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.240
'Cause they were like, "Oh,
you're just a dumb actress"

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.500
as opposed to like, "No, I'm smart

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:41.760
and totally capable of this job."

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.770
And it wasn't until like, I mean,

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.840
two years I was doing both jobs.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.150
And about two years in one
of my clients was like,

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:50.750
are you on "Young and the Restless"?

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:51.583
And I said, "No."

00:18:51.583 --> 00:18:52.980
- Through your voice or
they actually saw it?

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.900
- I think they saw it
and they saw the name

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.930
and they go there's a girl
on "Young and the Restless".

00:18:57.930 --> 00:18:58.920
I go, "I know that's not me.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:00.509
I don't know who that is.

00:19:00.509 --> 00:19:02.175
I don't know who that is."

00:19:02.175 --> 00:19:03.950
Like I tried to deny it as long as I could

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:06.530
'cause I didn't want them to
think I was a dumb actress.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:07.770
I was like the opposite of, I was like,

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:09.140
"I don't wanna publicize this.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.930
I don't want anybody knowing because-"

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.400
- "Because this is actually
paying my bills over here."

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:15.590
- Yeah, "It's gonna affect my job job.

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:17.180
And I don't want it to affect my job job."

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.540
And finally, the third year

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:20.031
being on "Young and the Restless"

00:19:20.031 --> 00:19:23.080
I was able to make enough
money to quit my job job.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.800
- When was that moment
that you knew you made

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.320
your parents proud with your career?

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:28.760
- Oh, so many.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:33.550
My mom and my dad, they've
been proud since I was in band

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:36.110
or I was a cheerleader
and they were proud.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.100
But I think when I got my star

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:39.350
on the Hollywood walk of fame,

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.820
that was something tangible
that they go, "Oh my God,

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:43.990
that's what happens to
big Hollywood people!"

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:46.000
And I'm like, "Yeah, I'm getting a star!"

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:49.060
And I got that star almost 20 years

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:51.276
to the day that I moved to LA.

00:19:51.276 --> 00:19:52.109
- Oh wow.

00:19:52.109 --> 00:19:53.960
- And I remember being
on Hollywood Boulevard

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:58.110
when I moved here in 1998 and
I went to Hollywood Boulevard

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:03.970
and I go, you know, one day
I'm gonna have a star here.

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:06.100
And it was like 20 years to the date

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:07.620
almost to the exact spot.

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:08.470
- You manifested it.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:10.005
- Yeah.

00:20:10.005 --> 00:20:11.830
And so they were really proud
of me in that moment as well.

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:13.820
And the other time they
were most proud of me

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:15.100
was when I got my masters.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:16.250
- I was gonna say, how did they feel?

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.153
'Cause you come from a family
that really values education.

00:20:18.153 --> 00:20:19.737
How did they feel when you told them

00:20:19.737 --> 00:20:22.100
I'm gonna go get my
masters in Chicano studies.

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:23.600
- My mom constantly bugged me about it.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:26.110
I'm the last person in my
family to get my masters.

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:26.943
- Wow.

00:20:26.943 --> 00:20:29.070
- Definitely, when my parents came here

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:30.717
for the graduation
ceremony, my mom was crying

00:20:30.717 --> 00:20:34.400
'cause she was like,
"Wow, it's important."

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:35.940
Education was very
important to our family.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.990
And so that was the other
finest moment for them.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:41.610
- I would love to talk about,

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:43.720
you've been directing for a while.

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:45.330
You did "Blackish", "Jane the Virgin"

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:46.710
a bunch of like different stuff.

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.910
As you were working for your
first feature directing role,

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:54.410
I was reading that you
really had to set yourself up

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:56.940
for that role for "Flamin' Hot".

00:20:56.940 --> 00:20:58.440
Can you walk me through that process?

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:01.830
I heard that that a couple
people kind of like helped you

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:02.910
along the way.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.136
- Oh, so many people, so many people.

00:21:05.136 --> 00:21:07.300
I've been directing for 10 years,

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.200
but this will be my first
narrative feature link

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.550
'cause I've done documentary features.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:13.760
So it's so funny.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:16.140
Like you never know you're
not qualified for something

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:17.855
until you go in for a job
and somebody tells you,

00:21:17.855 --> 00:21:19.600
"Well, you haven't done it yet!"

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.900
Like "Yeah, well I haven't
done it until you do it."

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:23.480
- Exactly.

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:26.430
- So I had to work twice as
hard just to get into the door,

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:28.079
twice as hard on my presentation,

00:21:28.079 --> 00:21:29.795
twice as hard on my pitch,

00:21:29.795 --> 00:21:32.060
and twice as hard on my vision.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.810
So I was practicing my pitch over and over

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:34.643
and over and over.

00:21:34.643 --> 00:21:37.010
I was practicing my pitch
with my friend, Brian Tanen

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:38.540
who's a show runner, big show runner.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.336
And I said, "Okay, Brian,
I'm gonna, do my pitch."

00:21:40.336 --> 00:21:43.150
And so I started and
not even one minute in,

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:44.620
he goes, "Okay, I gotta stop you.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:45.590
I gotta stop you right there."

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:46.710
And I was like, "Yeah?"

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.620
And he goes, "I want you walk out the door

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:53.940
and come back in with your
white male privileged pants on.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:55.500
You're gonna come into this room

00:21:56.413 --> 00:21:57.790
and assume you have the job.

00:21:57.790 --> 00:21:59.613
You're not coming into this
room and asking for the job.

00:21:59.613 --> 00:22:00.650
You're not coming into
this room and begging like,

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:01.900
'Oh please, please give it to me.'

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.280
You're coming in and saying,
'When I direct this movie,

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.450
this is what I'm gonna do.

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:07.920
My vision for this movie
is going to be this.'"

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:11.030
And it changed not only that pitch,

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:14.020
but every job going forward with that,

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:15.080
it changed in my life.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:17.240
The way I approach anything that I want.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:20.030
White male privilege is alive and well.

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.780
Any man that comes into that room is like

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:23.640
assumes he has the job,
of course he has a job.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.330
You'll be silly not to have me.

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:28.450
And it really just like
something clicked in my head.

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.140
And I went into that room
and I had all the confidence

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:31.973
in the world.

00:22:31.973 --> 00:22:33.920
I had every answer, I had every idea.

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:37.650
I had every piece all put
together and presented in a way

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:39.900
that I had to make it undeniable.

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.130
That's the only chance
I had, I was a woman.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.280
I was a woman of color.

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:47.170
I was first time director,
like everything's against me.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:48.280
So what do I gotta lose?

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:50.590
And it worked and I got the job.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:53.630
Being underestimated is
the best position to be in.

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:55.070
It's the best position to be in.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:56.680
Like, let's go prove ourselves.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:58.000
We have to prove ourselves.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.960
And so every day that
I showed up during prep

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.937
and on the set and the
filming of that movie

00:23:02.937 --> 00:23:04.735
and the editing is like 110%

00:23:04.735 --> 00:23:09.181
because I have to prove that
I can do this to people.

00:23:09.181 --> 00:23:10.014
I know I can do it,

00:23:10.014 --> 00:23:12.490
but I gotta prove to
everybody else that I can.

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.650
- Talk about your production company

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:17.500
and now being in a place
where you're consciously

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:20.333
hiring women in minorities, as opposed to

00:23:20.333 --> 00:23:23.534
what's been going on
which is unconsciously.

00:23:23.534 --> 00:23:25.343
Because a lot of people are like,

00:23:25.343 --> 00:23:26.176
"Oh, well we have this problem.

00:23:26.176 --> 00:23:27.410
How do we get here?"

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:29.370
And it's like, okay, one of
the reasons why we got here

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:30.660
is because people just the people

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:31.493
that they're used to working with.

00:23:31.493 --> 00:23:34.110
So we have to be really
conscious about switching that.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:37.340
- By the way, I don't think it's like,

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.460
I don't think all of
it is systemic racism.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:41.260
I think a lot of it is unconscious bias.

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:44.160
A lot of it is, "I've worked
with Bill and Tom and Bob.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.430
I'm gonna hire Bill and Tom and Bob."

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:47.263
- 'Cause you're familiar.

00:23:47.263 --> 00:23:48.096
- "I'm familiar with them.

00:23:48.096 --> 00:23:49.440
I know they can do it."

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.540
I remember producing "Grand Hotel"

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:54.102
and they gave me a list of DPs

00:23:54.102 --> 00:23:54.935
and they're like oh, here you go.

00:23:54.935 --> 00:23:56.880
And it was Bob and Bill and Tom.

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:59.490
And I was like, "Do you
have any female DPs?"

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:03.530
They go, "Oh yeah, yeah,
actually we'll get back to you."

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:06.552
And they brought me five
names and I hired a female DP.

00:24:06.552 --> 00:24:08.900
They weren't against the idea.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:11.150
They weren't like absolutely not.

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:15.750
They were unconsciously ignoring that.

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:18.360
So my production company,
our mission is to produce

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:20.030
with purpose, specifically,

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.990
building a pipeline of talent in front of

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:24.380
and behind the camera of Latinos.

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:26.350
We exist, we're here,
we're in this industry.

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:30.440
So I want to be able to
create those job opportunities

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:31.900
for them and give them that experience.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.574
So when they go to the next job, they go,

00:24:33.574 --> 00:24:35.228
"I have done this.

00:24:35.228 --> 00:24:37.296
I've already done this,
I know how to do this."

00:24:37.296 --> 00:24:39.370
And so that's the greatest
thing about being a producer

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:40.440
is job creation.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.810
Like I remember we were on
the set of "Telenovela",

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.434
my sitcom and the sets were going up.

00:24:45.434 --> 00:24:48.530
The construction crew
was putting up the sets

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.440
and I was walking the
sets with a friend of mine

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:51.980
and he goes, "Isn't this crazy?

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:56.010
You had an idea and now
300 people have a job."

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.300
And I was like, oh my God, yeah.

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:01.500
I mean, everybody from
construction to your crew

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:05.960
to dry cleaning, to
catering, the way one show

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:09.630
affects the economy and affects
people's economic mobility

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:13.300
is really fascinating to me
and so I enjoy that part of it.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:16.370
- When I see a show like
"Gentefied" be canceled.

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:19.795
When I see a show like "One
Day at a Time" be canceled.

00:25:19.795 --> 00:25:21.220
I feel like, okay, we're
taking steps forward.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.010
But maybe the shows aren't necessarily

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.210
getting the promotional
push or other things

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:28.160
to really be around for
seasons and seasons.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.770
How do you think we can
take that next step?

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:32.420
- There's so many factors to those things.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:33.810
I have been on TV shows
that have been canceled.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.100
I produced "Telenovela", we got canceled.

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:38.540
I produced "Grand Hotel", we got canceled.

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:41.480
I come from the mindset of
build it and they will come.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.890
If we give a show to the Latino audience,

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:46.640
they're gonna show up and
that has not been the case.

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:50.970
And why aren't they showing
up has a number of issues

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:51.803
connected to that.

00:25:51.803 --> 00:25:53.820
It's the same reason why we don't vote.

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:56.390
Are we not engaging in our
audience the way we should be?

00:25:56.390 --> 00:25:58.440
Are we not welcoming them to the platforms

00:25:58.440 --> 00:25:59.510
that these shows are on?

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:01.730
Are we not telling them
when it's on and how it's on

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:03.300
and where they can watch it?

00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:05.560
And at the same time,
are they not showing up

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.540
because "Gentified" is about
a Mexican-American family

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:11.170
and "I'm Argentinian, I don't get it."

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.270
We're a very factioned group.

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:14.540
We're not monolithic.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:17.950
And I think our community
focuses a lot on the differences

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:20.480
because we wanna prove
that we're not monolithic,

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:24.180
but I love the idea of like,
finding our commonalities.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.790
We have themes within our
community that are universal.

00:26:27.790 --> 00:26:31.450
Family, tradition,
culture, food, language.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.259
I mean, we all speak Spanish.

00:26:33.259 --> 00:26:34.950
I think there's a little bit of ownership

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:36.620
on our audiences to show up.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:39.490
If something is out there for us show up,

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:42.050
I don't care what background
you are, support it.

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:43.530
And then at the same time, I agree.

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:46.540
A lot of times we don't get
enough bites at the apple.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:49.240
So we get one "Gentified" a year.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.330
But why don't we have 10 shows on the air?

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:52.540
And if one gets canceled,

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:55.625
it's not such a casualty to our hearts.

00:26:55.625 --> 00:26:59.113
A lot of it's educating the
gatekeepers of our industry,

00:26:59.113 --> 00:27:01.000
who are mostly white male.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.507
And so we have to say, "No, no, no.

00:27:02.507 --> 00:27:04.680
'La Llorona' is gonna be a
really great, scary movie.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.190
You it's gonna resonate with
everybody, not just Latinos."

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:10.440
And especially with the
way media's consumed today

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:13.850
and made today, there's so many
options and so many outlets.

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:16.080
The only way that you're
gonna break through

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:19.100
as a new show or a movie
is through innovation.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:23.080
And the only way innovation
happens is by diversity.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:25.710
And so you can't keep going
back to that same talent pool

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:28.930
for the same story, 'cause
you're gonna get the same movie.

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:32.060
But you can still do Romeo
and Juliet through the eyes

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:33.800
of a Latino as a very different story.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:36.720
You can do Hamlet through
the eyes of a woman,

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:38.090
it will be very different.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:39.670
Not only do we have to
educate the gatekeepers,

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:41.260
we gotta change out the gatekeepers

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:43.090
and get us a seat at those tables.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:44.190
- For sure.

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:48.720
You've accomplished so much
already on the acting side,

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:50.020
directing side, producing side,

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:52.986
entrepreneurial side,
activism side, political side,

00:27:52.986 --> 00:27:55.360
what's left for you to accomplish?

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:56.860
Where does your focus go?

00:27:57.830 --> 00:27:59.120
- Oh my gosh, on all of it.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.300
I'm gonna continue to do all of it.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:04.040
So I still have so much I wanna act in,

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:06.690
I still have so much, I
wanna direct and produce.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:09.810
Political activism will never
go away as our communities

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:12.110
are still underserved
and underrepresented.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.900
My foundation's gonna be my life's work.

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.230
That's gonna be my work forever.

00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:20.230
And I think, being a mom has
really prioritized my life.

00:28:21.833 --> 00:28:23.470
He's the center of my universe.

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:26.520
So everything you now
revolves around that priority

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:31.290
and motherhood and so I
wanna continue to do it all.

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:33.660
- The last question that
I have and is a question

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:36.230
that we've been talking about
in our communities for a while

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:37.580
in terms of waking up the giant.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:41.920
We've 60 million US Latinos,
yet we don't necessarily have

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:45.110
the political capital that
should come with that.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:48.830
As someone who's been advocating
for the last 20 years,

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:52.265
what are some of the keys in order to-

00:28:52.265 --> 00:28:53.580
- Yeah, I mean, I think
it's voter education.

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:57.830
I mean, I never say I speak
for the Latino community.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:01.010
I always go out and encourage
the Latino community

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:02.310
to speak for itself.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:04.850
And the way you speak up
for yourself is by voting,

00:29:04.850 --> 00:29:07.260
by participating, civic engagement,

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.060
being involved, being informed.

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:11.390
We did a survey of if people
even knew the district

00:29:11.390 --> 00:29:13.060
they're in, "Do you know
what district you're in?"

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:14.710
And they're like, "Oh God, no."

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:17.640
"Do you know who your
local representation is?"

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:20.666
And I think a big misconception
for our communities is that

00:29:20.666 --> 00:29:23.240
every four years is a general
election for president

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:26.720
and they participate but
what really them day to day

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.850
is their local government.

00:29:28.850 --> 00:29:30.280
Who is on your school board,

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:31.710
who is on your city council?

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:35.450
That is what's going to affect
your everyday experiences.

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:37.460
People go, "I can't solve world hunger."

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.080
No, but you could donate
to your local food bank.

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.113
So that homeless guy that
you see at your coffee shop

00:29:43.113 --> 00:29:44.590
every morning, you could help him.

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:45.830
Because I think the misconception is

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:47.498
you have to be a
politician to be political

00:29:47.498 --> 00:29:49.440
and that's not the case.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:53.220
The greatest power in our
democracy is in the people,

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:54.600
is in the person, is in the vote.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:57.738
And so, continuing to
tell our community that.

00:29:57.738 --> 00:30:00.430
- I can't wait till the day
that we have a Latina president.

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:02.240
- I know, that would not be fun.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:07.100
We'll paint the white
house pink, Rosa Mexican.

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:08.390
- I love it, I love it.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:11.060
Thank you so much for your
time and for everything you do.

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:13.260
- Thank you, thank you
for my shitty coffee.

00:30:14.699 --> 00:30:15.578
(laughs)

00:30:15.578 --> 00:30:16.838
- I will take all the credit for that.

00:30:16.838 --> 00:30:19.421
(upbeat music)

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:22.923
It's a wrap.

00:30:23.787 --> 00:30:25.160
Thank you to Eva Longoria
for taking us on a trip

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:26.949
down memory lane.

00:30:26.949 --> 00:30:29.000
I really appreciate it that
we're actually on campus

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:31.620
where she went back to get her
masters in Chicano studies.

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:33.890
I feel like it has a lot
to do with the purpose

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:35.330
that she has for life.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:37.110
I think that's what I
resonate with the most.

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:39.770
The fact that she's using her
power to give our community

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.550
a voice, that's what the series is about.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:44.770
These are the people who are
using the spotlight on them

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:46.070
and actually flipping the script

00:30:46.070 --> 00:30:48.390
and using it to tell
stories of our community,

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:49.990
tell the stories of people whose voices

00:30:49.990 --> 00:30:52.440
we don't get to hear that
often in the mainstream media.

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:53.700
And that's what inspires me.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.440
And hopefully that's
what inspires you too.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:57.630
And hopefully that's what
we keep doing in the series

00:30:57.630 --> 00:30:59.077
until next time.

00:30:59.077 --> 00:31:01.660
(upbeat music)

